nostics = []\n    for c in calls:\n        if not isinstance(c, dict):\n            raise ValueError(\"each call must be a dict\")\n        # Check required fields\n        for key in ('call_id', 'loss_without_call', 'loss_with_call', 'prediction_tokens', 'parseable'):\n            if key not in c:\n                raise ValueError(f\"call missing field '{key}'\")\n        call_id = c['call_id']\n        loss_without = c['loss_without_call']\n        loss_with = c['loss_with_call']\n        tokens = c['prediction_tokens']\n        parseable = c['parseable']\n        # Validate types\n        if not isinstance(call_id, str):\n            raise ValueError(\"call_id must be string\")\n        if not isinstance(loss_without, (int, float)) or not isinstance(loss_with, (int, float)):\n            raise ValueError(\"losses must be numeric\")\n        if not isinstance(tokens, int) or tokens < 1:\n            raise ValueError(\"prediction_tokens must be integer >=1\")\n        if not isinstance(parseable, bool):\n            raise ValueError(\"parseable must be boolean\")\n        # Compute gain\n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\n        normalized_gain = gain / tokens\n        rounded_gain = round(normalized_gain, 8)  # arbitrary precision?\n        # Check acceptance condition\n        if parseable and normalized_gain >= threshold:\n            accepted.append(call_id)\n        # Append diagnostic\n        diagnostics.append({\n            \"call_id\": call_id,\n            \"normalized_gain\": rounded_gain\n        })\n    return {\n        \"accepted_call_ids\": accepted,\n        \"diagnostics\": diagnostics\n    }\n\nBut note: \"rounded per-call diagnostics\" might imply rounded the normalized_gain. We'll round to 8 decimal places. Could also round to the same precision as input? Input has 8 decimal places typically. So that's fine.\n\nAlso, potential requirement: \"Compute aggregate values from the returned primitive decisions rather than from a second approximation.\" Not clear. But we are not computing aggregate.\n\nThus, output should be a JSON-serializable dict with those two keys.\n\nNow, produce the JSON response. The entire response must be a single JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value being the Python source string. The Python source must be properly escaped for JSON: backslashes, quotes, newlines. We'll use triple quotes inside? Actually just escape newlines as \\n, quotes as \\\", backslashes as \\\\. We'll write the code as a string and then JSON-encode it. Since we are generating directly, we can manually escape.\n\nBetter to write the code as a raw string and then replace newlines with \\n etc. But easier: I'll write the code with newlines and then use json.dumps on the string. But I'm limited to text output. I'll manually escape.\n\nI'll produce: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    # ... code ...\\n    return ...\"}\n\nMake sure no Markdown fences.\n\nAlso, the function must be named solve and take one argument case. Return a JSON-serializable object (dict). Ensure no external dependencies.\n\nThus, final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1762,"completion_tokens":2426,"total_tokens":4188,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1664},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1883},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1664,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":98},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}